-- Q11: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry of management about control design","Observation of the general control environment","Tests of controls such as reperformance or inspection","Review of the client''s organizational chart"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): When the auditor plans to rely on controls, AU-C 330 requires testing operating effectiveness through tests of controls. This typically involves reperformance, inspection of documents showing the control operated, or a combination of procedures.
Wrong (A): Inquiry alone is insufficient to test operating effectiveness under AU-C 330.08; it must be combined with other procedures.
Wrong (B): Observing the general control environment does not constitute a test of controls over specific assertions.
Wrong (D): Reviewing an organizational chart provides understanding of the entity but does not test whether controls are operating effectively.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11;

-- Q16: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It requires no statistical knowledge from the auditor","It stratifies by dollar amount, giving larger items higher selection probability","It eliminates the need for understanding the client''s business operations","It produces results equivalent to a complete population examination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Monetary unit sampling selects individual dollars as the sampling unit, so larger account balances contain more sampling units and have a proportionally higher chance of selection. This built-in stratification focuses the audit on high-dollar items without requiring a separate stratification step (AU-C 530.A13).
Wrong (A): MUS requires statistical knowledge for proper application and evaluation of results.
Wrong (C): Understanding the client''s business is always required regardless of sampling method chosen.
Wrong (D): No sampling method produces results identical to a 100% examination of the population.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 16;

-- Q20: Audit Reports [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Unmodified opinion with no modification required","Qualified opinion due to material misstatement","Adverse opinion on the financial statements","Disclaimer of opinion due to the disagreement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 705.08, when misstatements are material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate. Misclassification of long-term debt as current is material but typically confined to balance sheet presentation and does not pervade the financial statements as a whole.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate when management refuses to correct a material misstatement.
Wrong (C): An adverse opinion under AU-C 705.09 is reserved for misstatements that are both material and pervasive to the financial statements.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer under AU-C 705.10 applies to scope limitations, not identified misstatements that management refuses to correct.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 20;

-- Q27: Quality Management [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Marketing and business development standards for the firm","The firm''s risk assessment process for identifying quality risks","Client profitability analysis procedures and benchmarks","Staff compensation benchmarking against industry data"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): SQMS No. 1 requires firms to establish a risk-based approach to quality management, including a risk assessment process that identifies quality risks and designs responses to address them. The eight required components include governance, ethical requirements, acceptance and continuance, engagement performance, resources, information and communication, and monitoring and remediation.
Wrong (A): Marketing and business development are not components of the quality management system under SQMS No. 1.
Wrong (C): Client profitability analysis is a business decision, not a quality management component.
Wrong (D): Staff compensation benchmarking is an HR function, not a required quality management component.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 27;

-- Q97: Audit Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It has no effect because each audit stands independently","The auditor should plan procedures to evaluate going concern status","The auditor should decline the engagement entirely","The auditor should automatically issue a going concern opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A prior going concern qualification is a significant planning consideration under AU-C 570. The current auditor should assess whether the conditions that led to the previous qualification still exist and plan procedures to evaluate the entity''s current ability to continue as a going concern.
Wrong (A): While each audit is independent, prior findings inform planning considerations and cannot be ignored.
Wrong (C): A prior going concern opinion does not require declining the engagement; it requires additional planning.
Wrong (D): Each audit requires independent evaluation — a prior going concern does not predetermine the current opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 97;

-- Q101: Risk Assessment [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They move in the same direction as components of audit risk","Detection risk is always set higher than the assessed misstatement risk","They are entirely unrelated concepts within the audit risk model","They have an inverse relationship within the audit risk model"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A2 and the audit risk model (AR = RMM x DR), detection risk has an inverse relationship with the risk of material misstatement. When assessed RMM increases, the auditor must decrease detection risk by performing more extensive substantive procedures to maintain overall audit risk at an acceptably low level (AU-C 330.07).
Wrong (A): A direct relationship would mean less testing when risk increases, contradicting the audit risk model.
Wrong (B): Detection risk is not always higher; it is set based on the assessed level of RMM.
Wrong (C): The two risks are mathematically related through the audit risk model, not independent of each other.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 101;

-- Q104: Risk Assessment [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confirming accounts receivable balances with third-party customers","Inquiring of management about risks and performing analytical procedures","Recalculating depreciation expense for all of the fixed assets","Counting inventory on hand at the client''s primary warehouse"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.06, risk assessment procedures include inquiries of management and others, analytical procedures, and observation and inspection. These help the auditor understand the entity and identify risks of material misstatement at the financial statement and assertion levels.
Wrong (A): Sending confirmations is a substantive procedure performed in response to assessed risks under AU-C 330, not a risk assessment procedure.
Wrong (C): Recalculation is a substantive test of details, not a risk assessment procedure.
Wrong (D): Physical inventory counts are substantive procedures designed to test assertions, not to identify risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 104;

-- Q111: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Completeness of recorded receivables","Existence and accuracy of receivables","Classification of receivable balances","Presentation of receivable disclosures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Accounts receivable confirmations primarily test the existence assertion (do the receivables actually exist?) and accuracy (are the amounts correct?). Under AU-C 505, confirmations sent to recorded customers verify that the balances are real and properly stated.
Wrong (A): Completeness testing requires a different direction of testing, such as tracing from shipping documents to the receivables ledger.
Wrong (C): Classification relates to whether receivables are properly categorized, which confirmations do not directly address.
Wrong (D): Presentation concerns how receivables are displayed in the financial statements, not whether individual balances exist.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 111;

-- Q112: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing recorded costs to current market prices for NRV testing","Observing the physical inventory count at year-end","Sending confirmation requests to the major suppliers","Tracing inventory purchases to the general ledger entries"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Valuation testing for inventory focuses on whether inventory is properly valued at the lower of cost or net realizable value under ASC 330. Comparing recorded costs to current market prices directly tests the valuation assertion.
Wrong (B): Physical observation tests existence, not valuation, and the stem states existence has already been confirmed.
Wrong (C): Confirmations to suppliers primarily address accounts payable assertions, not inventory valuation.
Wrong (D): Tracing purchases to the general ledger tests completeness and accuracy of recording, not the valuation assertion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 112;

-- Q114: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To replace the need for performing other audit evidence procedures","To confirm management''s responsibility and document key representations","To serve as the primary basis for issuing the audit opinion","To guarantee that the financial statements are completely accurate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 580.06, the management representation letter confirms management''s responsibility for the financial statements and formalizes oral representations made during the audit. It complements but does not replace other audit evidence.
Wrong (A): The letter supplements other evidence rather than replacing it; the auditor must still perform substantive procedures.
Wrong (C): The audit opinion is based on the totality of evidence gathered, not primarily on the representation letter.
Wrong (D): Management cannot guarantee accuracy — the letter acknowledges responsibility, not perfection of the financial statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 114;

-- Q116: Audit Sampling [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Statistical sampling is always more effective than nonstatistical methods","Statistical sampling uses random selection and probability theory for evaluation","Nonstatistical sampling always requires larger sample sizes than statistical","Statistical sampling can only be applied to tests of controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.05, statistical sampling uses mathematical probability theory to determine sample sizes and evaluate results, requiring random selection. Nonstatistical sampling relies on auditor judgment for both sample size determination and evaluation of results (AU-C 530.A6).
Wrong (A): AU-C 530.A1 clarifies that both methods can provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence when properly designed — neither is inherently superior.
Wrong (C): Sample size depends on multiple factors; nonstatistical sampling does not always require larger sizes.
Wrong (D): Both statistical and nonstatistical sampling can be used for tests of controls and substantive tests.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 116;

-- Q117: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The actual rate of deviations discovered in the sample tested","The expected rate of deviations existing in the overall population","The maximum deviation rate acceptable while still relying on the control","The probability that the sample results are statistically incorrect"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): The tolerable rate is the maximum deviation rate from a prescribed control that the auditor will accept without modifying planned reliance on the control. It is set during planning based on the planned degree of reliance and represents the auditor''s threshold for acceptable control performance (AU-C 530.A3).
Wrong (A): The actual rate of deviations found in the sample is the sample deviation rate, a result of testing rather than a planning input.
Wrong (B): The expected population deviation rate is a separate estimate used in determining sample size, not the tolerable rate.
Wrong (D): This describes sampling risk, which is the risk that the auditor''s conclusion differs from the true population characteristic.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 117;

-- Q118: Audit Sampling [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The population contains an excessively large number of items","The population is arranged in a completely random order","A cyclical pattern in the population coincides with the sampling interval","The computed sample size is smaller than initially expected"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530.A14-.A15, systematic selection can produce a biased or unrepresentative sample if the population contains a pattern coinciding with the sampling interval. For example, if every 10th invoice is a month-end adjusting entry and the interval is 10, the sample would always include or exclude these entries.
Wrong (A): A large population size does not make systematic selection unreliable; it works effectively regardless of population size.
Wrong (B): A randomly ordered population actually enhances systematic selection by approximating random sampling.
Wrong (D): A smaller sample size affects precision but does not make the systematic selection method itself unreliable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 118;

-- Q119: Audit Sampling [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The account balance is fairly stated as recorded by the client","The results do not support the recorded amount, requiring further procedures","The account must be written down by the full projected misstatement amount","The auditor should issue a qualified opinion on the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.13, when projected misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement, there is an unacceptably high risk that the account is materially misstated. AU-C 530.14 requires the auditor to consider expanding the sample, performing additional substantive procedures, or requesting management to investigate and adjust.
Wrong (A): Accepting the balance when projected misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement would be inappropriate.
Wrong (C): The auditor cannot unilaterally write down the account; management must investigate and propose adjustments.
Wrong (D): The opinion decision is made later after evaluating all accumulated evidence, not immediately from one sampling result.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 119;

-- Q122: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately inform management that an error has been detected","Conclude that the financial statements are materially misstated overall","Adjust the financial statements for the full amount of the difference","Investigate the difference by performing additional audit procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 520.07, when analytical procedures identify a difference exceeding the acceptable threshold, the auditor must investigate by inquiring of management and obtaining corroborating evidence. AU-C 520.A18 states the investigation should determine whether the difference has a plausible explanation or indicates a potential misstatement.
Wrong (A): Assuming the difference is an error without investigation is inappropriate; there may be a valid explanation.
Wrong (B): Concluding material misstatement based solely on one analytical procedure without further investigation is premature.
Wrong (C): Proposing an adjustment without first understanding the cause of the difference would be premature.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 122;

-- Q123: Substantive Procedures [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Send confirmations to vendors with large recorded payable balances","Review the aged accounts payable trial balance for unusual items","Vouch recorded payables to the corresponding receiving reports","Examine post-year-end disbursements and trace to the year-end listing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.A47, testing completeness of accounts payable requires searching for unrecorded obligations. Examining cash disbursements after year-end is the most effective procedure because payments in the subsequent period may relate to liabilities existing at the balance sheet date but not recorded (AU-C 500.A22).
Wrong (A): Confirming existing payable balances only verifies recorded amounts and does not detect unrecorded liabilities.
Wrong (B): Reviewing the aged trial balance only covers items already recorded, not omitted payables.
Wrong (C): Vouching recorded payables tests existence and accuracy of items already in the records, not completeness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 123;

-- Q126: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To communicate a scope limitation that was imposed by management","To report an uncorrected misstatement identified in the financial statements","To disclose a disagreement with management about an accounting policy","To highlight a matter properly presented that is fundamental to users"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 706, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlights matters already properly presented or disclosed in the financial statements that the auditor believes are fundamental to users'' understanding, such as a significant change in accounting principle or going concern uncertainty.
Wrong (A): Scope limitations affect the opinion itself and result in qualified opinions or disclaimers, not emphasis-of-matter paragraphs.
Wrong (B): Uncorrected misstatements lead to opinion modifications under AU-C 705, not emphasis-of-matter paragraphs.
Wrong (C): Disagreements with management about accounting policies may lead to modified opinions, not emphasis-of-matter paragraphs.$EXPL$
