-- Q129: Review/Compilation Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The accountant did not audit or review and provides no assurance","The accountant obtained reasonable assurance about the statements","Management is solely responsible for detecting all fraud","The financial statements are presented fairly in all material respects"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 80.21, the standard compilation report must state that the accountant did not audit or review the financial statements and therefore does not express an opinion or provide any assurance. This clearly communicates the limited nature of the accountant''s involvement.
Wrong (B): Reasonable assurance is audit-level language — a compilation provides no assurance whatsoever.
Wrong (C): While management is responsible for the financial statements, the compilation report does not address fraud detection responsibility.
Wrong (D): "Presented fairly in all material respects" is the language of an audit opinion, not a compilation report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 129;

-- Q130: Review/Compilation Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the anomaly because reviews do not require further investigation","Inquire of management about the change and consider additional procedures","Issue a qualified review report based on the analytical finding alone","Withdraw from the engagement due to the identified discrepancy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 90.28, when analytical procedures identify unusual or unexpected relationships, the accountant should inquire of management to obtain explanations and corroborating information. AR-C 90.29 requires additional procedures if management''s response does not adequately address the concern.
Wrong (A): Ignoring the matter would violate the accountant''s responsibility to obtain limited assurance under SSARS standards.
Wrong (C): Issuing a qualified conclusion based solely on one analytical anomaly without first inquiring of management is premature.
Wrong (D): Withdrawal is a last resort and is not warranted before attempting to resolve the matter through inquiry.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 130;

-- Q132: Review/Compilation Engagements [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A preparation requires no report; a compilation requires a written report","There is no difference between these two engagement types","A compilation does not require understanding the client''s business","A preparation engagement provides limited assurance to users"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 70.05, a preparation engagement does not require a report — instead, each page must include a legend stating "No assurance is provided." Under AR-C 80.20, a compilation requires the accountant to issue a written compilation report. Neither engagement provides assurance.
Wrong (B): Preparations and compilations are distinct services with different reporting requirements under SSARS.
Wrong (C): Both engagement types require the accountant to have an understanding of the client''s business and applicable framework.
Wrong (D): A preparation engagement provides no assurance whatsoever; neither does a compilation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 132;

-- Q134: Attestation Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GAAS for audits of historical financial statements","AR-C standards governing compilations and reviews","SSAE attestation standards requiring evidence and an opinion","Government Auditing Standards (Yellow Book) exclusively"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): An examination of compliance is an attestation engagement governed by SSAE standards (AT-C sections). The CPA obtains sufficient appropriate evidence to express an opinion on whether the entity complied, in all material respects, with the specified requirements.
Wrong (A): GAAS governs audits of historical financial statements, not compliance examinations under attestation standards.
Wrong (B): AR-C standards govern compilations and reviews of financial statements, not attestation engagements.
Wrong (D): Government Auditing Standards may apply in addition to SSAE but do not exclusively govern compliance examinations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 134;

